<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)</h1><p class="st-meta">1527 CE – 1868 CE · capital Zagreb</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Croatian kingdom under the Habsburgs, reduced by Ottoman conquest to the 'remnants of the remnants' and later a centre of the Croatian national revival.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 1 January 1527 the Croatian nobles assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1527-election-in-cetin">Cetin</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Habsburg</a> as king, in return for his help against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. By the late 16th century Ottoman conquests had reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> to a narrow band around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlovac">Karlovac</a> and the coast, the reliquiae reliquiarum of the old kingdom.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was governed by a ban and its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-parliament">Sabor</a>, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-crown-of-saint-stephen">Lands of the Hungarian Crown</a>, while much of its territory was detached into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/military-frontier">Military Frontier</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. The victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a> in 1593 halted the Ottoman advance. Nobility, Catholic Church and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> dominated public life until the Illyrian movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljudevit-gaj">Ljudevit Gaj</a> revived Croatian in the 1830s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1848 Ban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-jelacic">Josip Jelačić</a> led Croatian forces against the Hungarian revolution. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-hungarian-settlement">Croatian-Hungarian Settlement</a> of 1868 joined the kingdom with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-slavonia">Kingdom of Croatia-Slavonia</a>, an autonomous part of the Hungarian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg)" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Triune_Kingdom_of_Croatia,_Slavonia_and_Dalmatia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1527 CE – 1868 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zagreb</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand I, Nikola Zrinski, Josip Jelačić</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Croatian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Croatia (1527-1868), Habsburg Croatia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg" aria-label="Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5058 563 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Croatia (Habsburg) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5378 819L5388 874M5378 819L5381 747M5378 819L5381 747M5378 819L5398 749M5378 819L5378 819"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5388 874h0M5381 747h0M5381 747h0M5398 749h0M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5378 819h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5378 819h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zagreb. <a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=kingdom-of-croatia-habsburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
